"faqs": [
{"q": "What age is best for a family safari?", "a": "There is no single right age, and families travel with children of all ages. Many find that from around six years old, children have the patience and curiosity to fully enjoy game drives. Younger children can certainly travel too, especially on a private safari where the pace is flexible. Some activities have their own age guidelines, which we will explain when planning."},
{"q": "Is a private safari better for families?", "a": "We believe so. With your own vehicle and guide, you set the pace, take breaks when needed and adapt the day to your children's energy levels. There is no pressure to stay quiet for the sake of other guests, and your guide can tailor explanations to younger listeners. Every Wildara safari is private as standard."},
{"q": "How long are the drives between parks?", "a": "Northern Tanzania is relatively compact. The drive from Arusha to Tarangire typically takes around two hours, and transfers between Lake Manyara, Karatu and the Ngorongoro Crater are fairly short. The journey into the Serengeti is longer, but it includes game viewing along the way. We plan family itineraries to keep driving days comfortable."},
{"q": "Are family safaris safe?", "a": "Safety is central to every safari we arrange. Game drives take place within the vehicle, with an experienced guide who knows the parks and animal behaviour well. Lodges are chosen with families in mind. As with any travel, sensible precautions apply, such as following your guide's advice and preparing for the sun and insects."},
{"q": "Can we combine a family safari with a beach holiday?", "a": "Yes, and it is a very popular choice. After the excitement of the safari, a few days on Zanzibar give everyone time to relax. The island offers warm water, sandy beaches and the historic streets of Stone Town to explore. Domestic flights from the northern circuit to Zanzibar can be arranged on request."}
],
