"faqs": [
{"q": "Do I need professional equipment?", "a": "Not at all. Many wonderful safari photographs are taken on smartphones and compact cameras. That said, if you enjoy photography, a camera with a zoom lens in the range of around 100 to 400mm will give you more flexibility for wildlife. A wider lens is useful for landscapes and scenes that show animals in their environment. We are happy to advise."},
{"q": "How does a private vehicle help photographers?", "a": "With a private vehicle, you decide how long to stay at a sighting and where to position for the best light and background. Your guide can turn off the engine to reduce vibration, and there are no other passengers moving around or wanting to leave. The pop-up roof provides an elevated, stable shooting platform with all-round views."},
{"q": "When is the best time of year for photography?", "a": "Each season has its strengths. The calving season from January to March offers green landscapes and dramatic predator action around Ndutu. The long dry season brings clear skies, animals gathering near water and the Mara River crossings. The green season brings rich colours, dramatic clouds and fewer vehicles. We will help you choose based on your priorities."},
{"q": "Can I fly a drone on safari?", "a": "Drones are heavily restricted in Tanzania, particularly within national parks and conservation areas, where flying them is generally prohibited without special authorisation. Regulations are strictly enforced, and bringing a drone into the country may involve additional permits. For a smooth journey, we recommend leaving drones at home and enjoying the view from the vehicle."},
{"q": "Are early starts really necessary?", "a": "For the best photographs, yes. The soft, warm light shortly after sunrise and before sunset is the most flattering for wildlife and landscapes, and animals are often most active at these times. A private safari means you can plan early departures and return later in the day, taking a rest during the harsh midday light."}
],
